<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro II of Brazil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pedro II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro II was the second and last emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> as a child in 1831 and ruling in his own right from 1840. His long reign was the most stable period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>, with competent fiscal management and victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>. He was deposed by a military coup in November 1889 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil.jpg" alt="Pedro II of Brazil" width="313" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady / Levin Corbin Handy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_II_of_Brazil_-_Brady-Handy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pedro was born on 2 December 1825 in the Palace of São Cristóvão in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, the seventh child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a> and Empress Maria Leopoldina, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. His father's abdication and departure for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1831 left the five-year-old prince as emperor and began a regency marked by political instability. Pedro I chose José Bonifácio de Andrada as guardian, and the governess Mariana de Verna, whom the boy called Dadama, was a surrogate mother to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ending the regency brought stability, though a group of courtiers led by Aureliano Coutinho gained influence over the shy young emperor. Between 1848 and 1852 he faced three crises. Illegal slave imports, banned in 1826 under a treaty with the United Kingdom, continued, and a British act of 1845 allowed British warships to seize Brazilian ships involved in the trade. The Praieira revolt in Pernambuco began on 6 November 1848 and was put down by March 1849. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eusebio-de-queiros-law">Eusébio de Queirós Law</a> of 4 September 1850 gave the government wide powers against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and by 1852 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> accepted that it had been suppressed.</p><p>In the 1850s Pedro advanced a program of conciliação, to reduce partisanship, and melhoramentos, material developments, as railways, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and steamship lines linked the country. He was also a patron of learning and in March 1840 became the first Brazilian photographer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>, which began in 1864 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> invaded Mato Grosso, lasted five years, and he devoted his energy to raising troops and warships, warning in 1866 that the war must end as honour demanded, whatever the cost.</p><p>He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1871 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1876, where the press called him the Yankee Emperor. In 1887 poor health took him to Europe, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 22 May 1888 he learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> had been abolished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1889 Pedro was overthrown in a coup led mainly by military elites who favoured a republic. Unwilling to provoke civil conflict, he accepted exile. His wife, Teresa Cristina, died three weeks after they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and he lived modestly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He never supported a restoration. He died of pneumonia on 5 December 1891. Historians have attributed the fall of the monarchy chiefly to his own indifference to its fate, and later historians have ranked him highly among Brazilian rulers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro II of Brazil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pedro II</b> studied languages including Hebrew, Sanskrit and Tupi, and corresponded with Louis Pasteur and Henry Wadsworth Longfellow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Days earlier, on 7 April, Brazil's first emperor, Pedro I, had abdicated, leaving his five-year-old son as Emperor <b>Pedro II</b>, and on 13 April the ship carrying him left the port of Rio de Janeiro.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>Marshal Deodoro da Fonseca led a coup in Rio de Janeiro that deposed Emperor <b>Pedro II</b> and proclaimed the Republic of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leaders declared Bahia independent only until <b>Pedro II</b> reached adulthood, a compromise reflecting divisions among them.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahia-republic">Bahia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>On 15 November 1889 army officers led by Marshal Deodoro da Fonseca overthrew Emperor <b>Pedro II</b>, a year after the abolition of slavery had cost the monarchy the support of planters.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-22.91,-43.21&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Rio de Janeiro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pedro II of Brazil"><image href="/maps/globes/g330s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3969 4455h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3969 4455h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-22.91,-43.21&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
